{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HhV8SDtVqr3r5b9izuq65D","type":"tool_result","content":"25:- a registry for what review leaves;\n26-- an acceptance run with a negative control;\n27-- an independent session that accepts in the owner's place.\n28-\n29-A practice that holds is a candidate for 495's own phases. One that fails is dropped together with\n30-the measurement that sank it, so that 495 does not inherit it. The cycle itself came out of such a\n31-measurement. The decisions that shape it are recorded in `specs/adr/`, like those of the product.\n32-\n33-## How the cycle differs from 495\n34-\n35-The cycle and 495 answer the same question, how to accept a change an agent made, and they do not\n36-answer it the same way. Each difference below is one of three kinds:\n37-- **interim**: it goes away once 495 drives its own changes;\n--\n50:    no implementation can satisfy, which today stops the change (`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`).\n51-- **Who reviews: under trial.**\n52-  - The cycle: every story gets two model reviewers, for two rounds, and a promise the code does not\n53-    keep blocks it.\n54-  - 495: a review is a role the frozen protocol may require (`required_reviews`, empty by default).\n55-    When one is required, a rejection or a blocking finding fails G5 (`src/domain/gates/g5.ts`).\n56-  - At stake: whether systematic review earns what it costs.\n57-- **Who accepts: under trial.**\n58-  - The cycle: the owner accepts or names a gap. In an unattended run, a fresh model session decides\n59-    in the owner's place, and the record says `origine: automate`.\n60-  - 495: G5 combines evidence, reviews and human decisions deterministically against the frozen\n61-    protocol. Acceptance follows from G5 by default, or from an IH-10 decision when the policy asks\n62-    for one. A model call never yields a human decision (`ADR-014`), and only a human undoes what a\n--\n149:goes to the registry, and after the last round it is put to the owner as a promise the code does not\n150-keep. The answer to a round cannot class it elsewhere: a bypass it does not answer as fixed by a\n151-commit it made, whether contested, registered or claimed fixed without one, holds the next round's\n152-gate as it stands. One it answers as fixed holds that gate too, until each reviewer of the next round\n153-has replayed its path on the head of the branch and found it closed: a commit alone does not show\n154-what it fixes. A bypass the last round sends back to step 2 holds, in the same way, the gate of the\n155-round that follows the reopening. A review step relaunched after a block resumes at the round that\n156-follows the last one answered, with the bypasses that answer left open or claimed fixed.\n157-\n158-A finding is located before it is handled. A defect the branch introduces or makes reachable belongs\n159-to it, even when the faulty line predates it; a defect it neither introduces nor makes reachable goes\n160:to the registry and does not hold the review back.\n161-\n162-The answer to a round fixes what is blocking or to be fixed. A suggestion whose fix adds no behaviour\n163-is fixed in the round; a suggestion whose fix would add a refusal, a state or a mechanism goes to the\n164:registry. A fix that adds a mechanism is designed before it is put in: where the state is born, who\n165-reads it, how many entries lead to it; removing a second entry is better than keeping it. A finding\n166-that touches only text is fixed without being reviewed by fresh reviewers: the coordinator checks the\n167-fix against its finding.\n168-\n169:The gate closes without a percentage. After the second round, what remains goes to the registry\n170:`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`, named as introduced by the branch when it is, except a promise the code\n171-does not keep, a bypass of the security section included, which is put to the owner: they decide\n172-between landing and a fix, which goes back through step 2 and one round on its diff.\n173-\n174-Records do not move during review. The revision, timestamps and test count of a record are set when\n175-the gate passes. A record lagging behind the reviewed revision is not a finding, any more than the\n176-subject of a branch commit, which never reaches `main`.\n177-\n178-## Preflight and defects found along the way\n179-\n180-Preflight is `npm run check`. It is green before every step, and before every commit that touches\n181-`src/`, `test/`, `scripts/`, `bench/`, `contracts/`, `README.md`, `NOTICE`, `LICENSE`,\n182-`package.json`, `package-lock.json`, a `tsconfig*.json` or `biome.json`. A story under\n--\n196:an entry in `specs/bugs/registry.yaml` and its own cycle. It is recorded without a fix only if its\n197-reproduction fails after a good-faith attempt. \"Already there before\", \"unrelated to the session\",\n198-\"out of scope\" are not answers.\n199-\n200:## Git and commits\n201-\n202-One branch per story or per fix cycle. Commit message: `<type>: <description>`, one line, in English,\n203-whatever the language of the session or of the file changed; the types are `feat`, `fix`,\n204-`refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`. The message states the resulting behaviour, never\n205-the process that produced it: no work-package reference, no review round, no attribution, no\n206-`Co-Authored-By`. Nothing is pushed by an automaton; `gh` for every GitHub operation, never the API\n207-directly; no issue is created by an automaton.\n208-\n209-## What is written by hand, and what is observed\n210-\n211-By hand: a product decision in `specs/adr/`, the order of the work in `specs/plan.yaml`, where\n212-the owner marks `prete: oui` the epics that run without them, and, for every epic added to the plan,\n--\n214:its stories reads. The story and the registry entry are\n215-written by hand when the owner drives the story; in a run without them, a session writes them under\n216-the rules of this file. Everything else is observed and recorded by the tool at the moment it\n217-observes it: a red and its message, a Preflight and its revision, a review round and its findings,\n218-the acceptance run and the owner's agreement, the landing. Evidence is written once, in the record of\n219-the step that produced it; elsewhere it is cited. A copy drifts from the code it describes.\n220-\n221-## The cycle without the owner\n222-\n223-`npm run cycle -- suite` runs, one after the other, the epics that `specs/plan.yaml` marks\n224-`prete: oui`, in the order of the plan. For each: the next story of the plan is written by a session\n225-when none is listed to do, it is driven to landing as above, the plan marks it landed, and the epic\n226-becomes `versé` when a session finds that its stories deliver its purpose. The run starts from `main`\n--\n234:  records in the registry, then decides `accepte` or `ecart` (`prompts/arbitrage.md`). For each\n235:  defect the branch records, it retains a severity and says why: the registry carries it, the record\n236-  keeps it beside the severity the branch wrote, and the tool stops, naming the defect, when one has\n237:  none, and naming the line, when the registry holds a line outside the form it reads. The owner,\n238-  when the story waits for them, sees those defects with their severity in the question. A gap is a\n239-  written promise or a security guarantee not kept, a case the previous code stopped and the branch\n240-  lets through, or a defect of the branch that weakens a guarantee of the story; otherwise the\n241:  agreement carries a note naming what remains in the registry and what the acceptance run did not\n242:  exercise. A defect the arbitration finds and the registry does not carry, it records there and\n243-  commits; the tool stops if it touches another file or leaves the tree modified. A product question\n244-  the story does not settle is settled by the behaviour that stops. The decision is recorded with\n245-  `origine: automate`.\n246-- **The gap.** Whether the arbitration names it or the acceptance run reopens it, a session writes it\n247-  into the story (a scenario, the security sentence, a task whose red is checked in the code) and\n248-  commits it before red-green starts again: without a written promise, review cannot judge the fix.\n249-  The tool reads the story again, requires that it gained a scenario or a task and that the tree is\n250-  clean.\n251-- **A promise review did not get kept.** It goes back to red-green: it was not the owner's to waive.\n252-\n253:**Registry defects** are fixed too, at the right time (`D-73`): at the end of each epic, before the\n254-next one, those of medium or high severity, whether the suite drove the epic or `cycle <story> auto`\n255-landed its last story; at the end of the run, the low ones. A session picks the first open defect\n256-that needs no product decision, writes its fix story under epic `e28` (which the plan never marks\n257:ready) citing the registry entry, and the tool drives it through the six steps; at landing, the\n258:entry moves from the registry to `specs/bugs/registry-fixed.yaml`, marked fixed at the landed\n259-revision. The defects the session sets aside because they need the owner are named at the end of the\n260-run, with the reason, without stopping it. One phase fixes at most `CYCLE_495_DEFAUTS_MAX` defects\n261-(5 by default); `npm run cycle -- defauts [severity]` runs that phase alone.\n262-\n263-The run stops, and hands back control, when a story has been sent back to red-green three times,\n264-when it has spent more than `CYCLE_495_PLAFOND_USD` ($80 by default), when a session does not return\n265-its output, or when a step blocks: a defect of the tool itself, a test that cannot be red, a red\n266-Preflight. Writing a story stops the same way on what it cannot write without choosing in the owner's\n267-place (`bloque`, with the choice named), and an epic with no possible first story stays the owner's.\n268-\n269-## Refactorings\n270-\n--\n296:saying in `Limite` what it does not do, and the rest goes to the registry or to a story.\n297-`CYCLE_495_REPRISES_MAX` caps the number of refactorings in one run. Nothing is pushed.\n298-\n299-## The tool\n300-\n301-`npm run cycle -- <story>` drives the remaining steps, in order, until a step needs the owner or\n302-blocks; `npm run cycle -- <story> etat` says where it stands;\n303-`npm run cycle -- <story> suivre` follows, from another terminal, the story that is running;\n304-`npm run cycle -- suite` runs the epics marked ready, without the owner;\n305:`npm run cycle -- defauts [severity]` fixes the open defects of the registry, without running an epic;\n306-`npm run cycle -- reprises` drives the behaviour-preserving refactorings of `specs/reprises.md`;\n307-`npm run cycle -- <story> auto` drives a single story the same way;\n308-`npm run cycle -- <story> accepte [note]` records the agreement after the acceptance run;\n309-`npm run cycle -- <story> ecart \"<what is missing>\"` sends it back to red-green for the named gap,\n310-with a single review round on its diff. The record lives under `~/.495/cycle/<story>/` during the\n311-story (`CYCLE_495_DIR` moves it).\n312-\nbugs:\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-27T170100\n    date: \"2026-09-27\"\n    title: \"G1 accepts a mandatory requirement's satisfied_by_reference claim without evidence, before G2/G5 are known to catch a false one\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n      A material answer bound to a mandatory requirement passes G1 (`gateG1`, `src/domain/change/decide.ts`)\n      as soon as the requirement is named, is mandatory, and the binding is observable — G1 never reads\n      `satisfied_by_reference`. A specification report can mark a brand-new, never-implemented\n      requirement `satisfied_by_reference: true` (a claim reserved for behaviour the project already\n      honours, per the `specify` role's own instructions) and still clear G1 unchallenged.\n    what_expected: >\n      A material answer resolved only by a false `satisfied_by_reference` claim should not clear a gate\n      on the model's word alone — some control downstream must be known to catch it, or G1 itself must\n      refuse it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Write a specification report whose mandatory requirement is marked `satisfied_by_reference: true` for behaviour the reference tree does not have, with a material answer bound to it.\"\n      - \"Advance the change through G1: `gateG1` names no reason and passes.\"\n      - \"Whether `preparation.ts`'s discrimination against the reference (feeding G2) or G5's evidence review would still catch the false claim before integration was not traced in this pass.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `satisfied_by_reference` is read by `preparation.ts` (`~line 116`) to flag a requirement as\n      `undiscriminated` when the reference tree fails to exercise it, feeding a later gate's evidence —\n      but `gateG1` (`~decide.ts:505-515`) never consults it before passing the requirements document, so\n      a false claim is not refused at the gate meant to bind an answer to a real obligation; whether it\n      is refused later depends on control paths this pass did not trace to a verdict.\n    evidence: >\n      Read during the round-1 review of e01s03 (`specs/verifications/e01s03-relecture.md`, P2):\n      `gateG1`'s mandatory-answer loop names no check on `satisfied_by_reference`; `preparation.ts:116-126`\n      is the only reader found, and it is proved only by the tests that already pass, not by a new\n      failing one exercising a false claim. G2 and G5's own handling of the case was not traced.\n    reproduced: >\n      Reproduced on 2026-10-01 with a scripted agent on the reference npm target (`npm run campagne -- npm`).\n      A specification report whose only mandatory requirement asks for a function the tree does not have,\n      marked `satisfied_by_reference: true`, and an implementation that only appends a comment, pass G0 to G5\n      (unit, coverage and mutation PASS, `lastFit` absent from the candidate): `diagnoseControlCapability`\n      reports no undiscriminated requirement while the reference suite executes a test, so no failing test is\n      prepared, no arbitration is asked, and every control stays green on the reference and on the candidate.\n      The same report with `satisfied_by_reference: false` stops at \"no test can judge R1\" and waits for the\n      owner. G5 asks for a human acceptance only under `policy.g5_human_acceptance`, which is off by default.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Owner decision of 2026-10-01: no human confirmation, which would stop the automatic process, and no\n      mechanism now. The defect is carried by epic `e13`: a mandatory requirement the specification says the\n      reference already satisfies is proved by a characterization test that must pass on the reference, and a\n      claim that test refutes follows the path of a requirement asking for something new. Until then the gap\n      stays open and reproduced. The mixed case, where a prepared failing test for one requirement lifts the\n      check on every other, is read in the code and not reproduced. Since the binding of each requirement to its own\n      cases, a requirement declared satisfied by the reference whose cases contradict it is named at G2; one that no case\n      names (`no_case`), or for which no preparation ran, still rests on non-regression in `undecidedRequirements`\n      (`src/application/preparation.ts`) as soon as the reference suite executes a case, so the gap stays open on that path.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-28T103000\n    date: \"2026-09-28\"\n    title: \"environment.change and evidence.invalidate accept a closed change, and invalidating G5 there says a closed accepted change pending\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n    follow_ups: >\n      In `accord-campagnes.test.ts`, journal a campaign FAIL then PASS at the head and assert the agreement is accepted,\n      and PASS then FAIL and assert it is refused; the mutation `findLast` → `find` must make one of them fail.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-10T231500\n    date: \"2026-10-10\"\n    title: \"An unconfined cycle session can forge what the cycle tool reads back, its journal or the repository's git configuration, so no check of the tool holds against a session that cheats\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: cycle\n    what_happened: >\n      The cycle runs its sessions as Claude Code sessions with the whole repository and the user's home in reach. The\n      reviewers of e39s03 showed an acceptance session that leaves a detached process behind to append a forged\n      `controle campagne-maven PASS` line to `~/.495/cycle/<story>/journal.jsonl` after the session's own exit is\n      checked, and one that sets `git config core.fsmonitor` to a script the tool then runs through `git status`.\n      Each fix to such a path made the next round find another.\n    what_expected: >\n      A cycle session cannot write the story's record nor the repository's git configuration, so what the tool reads\n      back is what it wrote or observed.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Give the acceptance session of a story a fake claude that spawns a detached `sh -c 'sleep 1; printf <line> >> <journal>'` and exits.\"\n      - \"Accept the story: the forged line is read as a campaign the tool played.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The cycle's sessions run unconfined by design until 495's confined worker replaces them (`cycle/README.md`); the\n      tool's checks read files a session can write.\n    evidence: >\n      Found by the reviewers of e39s03 in attack mode on 2026-10-10. The owner bounded the attack to what a branch\n      exposes (`D-89`, précision du 2026-10-10) and left this to confining the sessions.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Confine the cycle's sessions so that they cannot write `~/.495/cycle/` nor `.git/config`, or replace them by 495's\n      confined Pi worker.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-10T151609\n    date: \"2026-10-10\"\n    title: \"The cycle writes into the registry the severity the arbitration retains without checking that it is low, medium or high, nor that a reason comes with it\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: cycle\n    what_happened: >\n      `arbitrer` (`cycle/src/automate.ts`) reads the `gravites` of the arbitration's output and `retenirGravites` writes\n      each `gravite` into `specs/bugs/registry.yaml` and the journal as it comes. Only the schema passed to\n      `claude --json-schema` limits it to `low`, `medium` or `high` and requires `raison`; the tool checks neither, and\n      the fake claude of the tests ignores the schema, so every mutant of that schema survives.\n    what_expected: >\n      A retained severity outside `low`, `medium` and `high`, or one without its reason, stops the story naming the\n      defect, as a defect with no retained severity does.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Give the arbitration of a story whose branch records one defect a fake claude that returns `gravites: [{ bug_id, gravite: \\\"critical\\\", raison: \\\"\\\" }]`.\"\n      - \"The agreement is recorded and the registry carries `severity: critical`, which `defautsOuverts` orders as low.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The output of the arbitration is cast to its type and trusted; the schema is the only check of its fields.\n    evidence: >\n      Introduced by the branch of the story e39s04. Found by mutation in the first review round (the mutants of the\n      schema of `gravites` survive).\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Refuse, in `arbitrer`, a retained severity outside the three the registry reads or without a reason, with a test\n      in `arbitrage-gravite.test.ts` where the arbitration returns one.\n    status: open","is_e